{"id":3240,"date":"2021-07-19T07:05:55","date_gmt":"2021-07-19T14:05:55","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3240"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:35:44","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:35:44","slug":"1-5-go-function","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-5-go-function\/","title":{"rendered":"1.5: The Go Function (Abstraction)"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22143f8ac8d\" name=\"6a22143f8ac8d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<h2><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">By the end of this lesson, you should be able to:<\/span><\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Incorporate your knowledge of creating a complete program from Island 1 to build a full flight path for the drone including takeoff and landing.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Utilize the go() method to make your drone fly to a specific location<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Explain what abstraction is and how it is used in programming<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Describe the benefits of abstraction in programming<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">In this lesson, you will complete the following steps. Make sure that you demonstrate your completed code to your instructor after each step:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Getting Started<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Multitasking Using Two Flight Commands<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">An Easier Way: The go() command<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Challenge 1: Puddle Jumper<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Challenge 2: Obstacle Course<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22143f8adb1\" name=\"6a22143f8adb1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting Started<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Before we learn how drones move, we need to have a program created for testing movement. To do this, follow the steps below:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Open Pycharm<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Right click on the <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">2_Flight_Directions <\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">folder you created in lesson 1 and click New -&gt; Python File<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Name the file\u00a0<em>multitasking<\/em><\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Enter the commands you learned from the previous lesson to pair with the drone, take off, and land. (Hint: You can go back to the previous lesson\u2019s folder to look at what you wrote if you can\u2019t remember)\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">You are now ready to start testing some of the movements that your drone can perform!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22143f8ae4b\" name=\"6a22143f8ae4b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Multitasking: Using Two Flights Commands at Once<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">As we learned in the Drone Tango, the drone can move in a single direction or multiple directions at once. Keep in mind that the drone cannot do two of the same command at once. For example, this code would result in the drone only moving backwards, not forwards.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.set_pitch(20)\r\ndrone.set_pitch(-20)\r\ndrone.move(1)<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">However, we can combine different flight commands to make the drone move in more complex directions such as flying at an angle instead of a straight line. To see an example of how this works, create a new file called <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">multiple.py <\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">and run the code below.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\ndrone.pair()\r\ndrone.takeoff() \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # takeoff for 2 seconds\r\ndrone.set_pitch(30)\u00a0 \u00a0 # Set positive pitch to 30% power\r\ndrone.set_roll(-30)\u00a0 \u00a0 # Set negative roll to 30% power\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\r\ndrone.move(2) \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # forward and left for 2 seconds\r\ndrone.land()\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # lands the CoDrone\r\ndrone.close() \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 # disconnects CoDrone<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;2827&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What you should have observed was the drone moving forward and to the left at the same time! Take a minute and play around with the different movement commands and see what you can do. See if you can answer the following when you are done:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What happens if you use pitch, roll, and yaw all at the same time?<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Can you combine throttle and pitch or roll?<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What if you wanted to make the drone fly in a semicircle, what commands would you combine?<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">What is the disadvantage of having to reset the movement values after each movement?<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22143f8af3d\" name=\"6a22143f8af3d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>An Easier Way: The go() Command<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The last question in the previous step asked you about the disadvantages of using flight commands for drone navigation. You may have noticed the following:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">It is easy to forget to reset the values.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">It makes it harder to separate movements<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">It requires extra lines of code<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">It is not very intuitive<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">When we see issues like extra code and difficulty to implement or understand, as programmers we want to apply a concept called <\/span><b>ABSTRACTION. <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Abstraction is the process of hiding extra or confusing features to reduce complexity and increase efficiency. So when we see, as a programmer, that commands are repetitive or difficult to use, we create new commands or features that make our code simpler to use. This is what the go() command does. It takes the code we learned from the individual flight commands and the move() command and <\/span><b>abstracts <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">\u00a0them into a single, easier to use command. This is what the function looks like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.go(direction, duration, power)<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">This is what each part means:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Direction: where the drone will fly. You can use FORWARD, BACKWARD, LEFT, RIGHT, UP, and DOWN, but make sure you type in all caps like you see here. You will also need to type Direction before it, so it will look like <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Direction.FORWARD<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Duration: how long the movement will happen in seconds. You can leave it as blank, zero, or any positive number. If duration is zero, it runs indefinitely. If duration has no number, it automatically sets to 1. If you put any positive number, duration is equal to that value.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Power: the speed of the drone. It can be any number between 0-100, with 0 being no power and 100 being full power. If power has no number, it will default to 50.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Here are some examples:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.go(Direction.UP, 3, 75) # go up for 3 seconds at 75% power\r\ndrone.go(Direction.FORWARD) \u00a0 # go forward for 1 second at 50% power\r\ndrone.go(Direction.LEFT, 6) \u00a0 # go left for 6 seconds at 50% power<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">If you want to use <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">drone.go()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">in your programs, you&#8217;ll need to include this line at the top, right underneath<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400\">import CoDrone_mini<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">from CoDrone_mini import Direction<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">This tells the program to look for and use the specific part of the CodDrone package called Direction because it is not used by default.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">After that, you&#8217;re good to go! Try using <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">drone.go()<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400\">in the program you wrote for Step 3 in place of<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400\">drone.set()<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400\">and<\/span> <span style=\"font-weight: 400\">drone.move()<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">.<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> What happened?<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Once you have that completed, try going back and redoing the box flights in Challenge 1. When you are done you should be able to answer the following questions:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is using the go() command easier than the set() commands for this challenge? Why or why not?<\/li>\n<li>Did you use fewer lines of code with the go() command instead of the set() commands?<\/li>\n<li>Do you think there is a time to use set() commands versus a time to use go() commands? If so, describe examples of when to use each set of commands.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22143f8afb2\" name=\"6a22143f8afb2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: Puddle Jumper<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_single_image image=&#8221;2834&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Have two tables or chairs 2 feet apart. The goal is to have the CoDrone Mini take off from one chair, land on the other, turn around, fly back, and then land at the starting point. It should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">A -&gt; B then B -&gt; A<\/span><\/p>\n<p><b>Rules:<\/b><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">The CoDrone Mini should always fly forward between chairs.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">If you succeed, move the tables or chairs an additional 2 feet apart. Continue adding 2 feet for every success until you reach the ends of the room.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Hint: You can use <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\">drone.land()<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400\"> in the middle of the code and take off again.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22143f8b022\" name=\"6a22143f8b022\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 2: Obstacle Course<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, work with a partner or a group of 3 to design an obstacle course. The course must contain between 5 and 10 obstacles and must be achievable with the commands you know so far. Once you have designed the course, work together to have your drone complete the course in the fastest time with the fewest lines of code.<\/p>\n<p>If you are doing this as a part of a class, complete the next steps:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Come up with a name for your group.<\/li>\n<li>Take a piece of paper and write your team name, best time, and number of lines of code and tape it or place it near the beginning of your obstacle course.<\/li>\n<li>Go and try to beat other team&#8217;s records for their obstacle courses. The teams with the least lines of code and the fastest time win!<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Rules:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Between 5-10 obstacles in course<\/li>\n<li>Can only use commands you already know<\/li>\n<li>Drones must complete all obstacles for a time to count<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 7 Goals and Steps to Success [vc_column_text] By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: Incorporate your knowledge of creating a complete program from Island&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/1-5-go-function\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4153,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3473,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[],"class_list":["post-3240","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","module-level-1","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3240","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":15,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3240\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":4208,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/3240\/revisions\/4208"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4153"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=3240"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=3240"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=3240"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}